course: everyday
lesson: 477
title: Saying sorry, and the sorry that is not accepted
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kieran calls Lily «Smelly Lily» in front of the class. His mum makes him say sorry, so he mumbles «soz» to the floor. Lily says, «That's not a real sorry.» Next day he finds her, looks at her and says, «I'm sorry I called you that. It was horrible and it's not true.» She accepts. A real sorry looks at the person, says what you did and why it was wrong.
explain: Some sorries aren't sorries at all. Mia writes Ava a note: «Sorry you got upset.» Ava gets even more upset, because that blames her for feeling bad. Mia's mom helps her write, «Sorry I said your drawing was ugly. It was mean and it wasn't true», and Ava sends back a heart. Jake says «Sorry, innit» to Sam with a big smirk while his mates snigger. Sam says, «Come back when you mean it.» Two days later, alone, Jake does. When Connor pushes Kieran in the dinner queue, Kieran's mum makes him say «it's fine» — but inside he is still angry, so it isn't fine at all. Chloe forgets Aisha's birthday, and instead of a quick text she bakes a lopsided cake with «SORY» iced on top; Aisha laughs and hugs her. After Maddie told Lily's secret, she says a proper sorry; Lily says, «I forgive you, but no secrets for a while.» A month later Lily tries a tiny test secret, Maddie keeps it, and trust starts coming back.
explain: Sometimes sorry needs a fix as well. Jake sits on Sam's new headphones and snaps them: «Sorry, mate.» «Sorry doesn't fix them, does it?» Jake pays half from his birthday money, and then Sam accepts. Kieran apologises to Mr Grimshaw for the greenhouse glass, and Mr Grimshaw says, «Apology accepted. You'll weed my veg patch every Saturday for a month.» By the fourth Saturday Kieran knows a carrot from a weed. And Sam says sorry to Mrs Hughes for cheek, and means it: «Thank you, Sam. That was brave. You've still got detention.» Next day she is friendly to him in the corridor.
explain: And sometimes the sorry is refused. Maddie apologises to Ellie for laughing when she fell in the mud; Ellie says, «I'm never forgiving you, ever.» Her mum says, «You did the right thing. She might need time, or she might never. That's her choice.» Lily's dad writes a long sorry letter to his brother after ten years; it comes back unopened. «I've done my part, love. The rest is up to him.» Saying sorry is your job; accepting it is theirs.
fact: A real apology looks at the person, says what you did, says why it was wrong, and has no excuses.
fact: «Sorry you got upset» is not a real apology, because it blames the other person's feelings.
fact: A sorry said with a smirk or a sarcastic voice is just another dig, and you are allowed to say so.
fact: When you break or damage something, a sorry plus making it right, like paying or fixing, is stronger than sorry alone.
fact: Saying sorry does not cancel the consequences, such as a detention, but it changes how people see you.
fact: The other person decides whether to accept a sorry; you cannot force forgiveness.
fact: Being made to say «it's fine» when it isn't does not mean you have really forgiven.
fact: Some apologies are refused, sometimes for years; you can only control your own part.
fact: Sometimes an action, like a home-made cake, says sorry better than words.
fact: You can forgive someone and still be careful with them for a while; trust comes back step by step.
fact: «Soz» is a quick, slangy way to say sorry, and often sounds like you don't mean it.
exercise: Why was Kieran's «soz» not a real sorry? | He mumbled it to the floor because his mum made him | It was far too long and full of excuses about Connor | He said it to the teacher | He wrote it down
exercise: What made Kieran's second sorry a real one? | He looked at Lily and said what he did was horrible and untrue | He gave her a present and a card, and said it in front of his mum | He said it louder | He said «soz» twice
exercise: What did Sam say when Jake said sorry for the headphones? | «Sorry doesn't fix them, does it?» | «It's fine, they were rubbish anyway, mate» | «I'll break yours too» | «Never speak to me again»
exercise: When did Sam accept Jake's sorry about the headphones? | When Jake helped pay | Straight away, as soon as he heard it | Never, not even a year later | When Jake cried in front of him
exercise: Ellie said she'd never forgive Maddie. What did Maddie's mum say? | You did the right thing, forgiving is her choice | Keep saying sorry every day until she gives in to you | You should never have apologised | Make Ellie forgive you
exercise: Kieran's mum made him say «it's fine» to Connor. Was it fine? | No, Kieran was still angry inside | Yes, everything was fine at once after he said it | Yes, because he said it | No, so he pushed Connor back
exercise: Why did «Sorry you got upset» make Ava more upset? | It blamed her feelings, not what Mia did | It had loads of spelling mistakes in it | It was too short | It was written in pencil on a scrap of paper
exercise: How did Chloe say sorry for forgetting Aisha's birthday? | She baked a lopsided cake saying «SORY» | She said sorry three more times, very loudly | She bought Aisha a phone | She sent a text with one emoji
exercise: Sam said sorry to Mrs Hughes and meant it. Did he still get detention? | Yes, but she was friendly to him the next day | No, a sorry like that cancels any detention | No, she gave him a prize | Yes, and she never spoke to him again
exercise: Jake said «Sorry, innit» with a smirk. What did Sam do? | Said «Come back when you mean it» | Accepted it straight away with a smile | Pushed Jake over | Told Jake's mum
exercise: What happened to Lily's dad's sorry letter? | It came back unopened | His brother wrote back at once | It got lost in the post | Lily's dad never sent it
exercise: What did Mr Grimshaw ask Kieran to do after his sorry? | Weed his veg patch on Saturdays | Pay a hundred pounds for the broken tomatoes | Never play football again in the street | Write a sorry letter every day
exercise: Maddie told Lily's secret. What did Lily say when Maddie said sorry? | «I forgive you, but no secrets for a while» | «It's fine, here's another secret, a bigger one» | «I'll never forgive you» | «Say it again louder»
exercise: How did Maddie earn back Lily's trust? | She kept a tiny test secret a month later | She told her own biggest secret to everyone in class | She bought her a present | She said sorry fifty times
exercise: What does «soz» usually sound like? | A quick sorry that may not be meant | A very deep, heartfelt sorry | A thank you | A goodbye
test: «I said sorry, so you HAVE to forgive me». True? | No, forgiving is the other person's choice | Yes, a sorry means they have to forgive you | Yes, if you say it three times | No, so don't bother saying sorry
test: «soz innit 😏». What's wrong with this sorry? | The smirk makes it another dig | Nothing, it's a perfect sorry, short and quick | It's too long | It has a spelling mistake
test: «Sorry if u got upset or whatever». Better version? | «Sorry I said your drawing was ugly. It was mean» | «Sorry u can't take a joke, it was only banter tho» | «Sorry but u started it» | «Whatever, I said sorry already»
test: Jake broke Sam's headphones. Jake says, «I said sorry, what more do you want?» What would make it right? | Help pay to replace them | Say sorry once more, louder | Nothing, sorry is always enough | Break something of his own
test: «I said sorry so why've I still got detention, that's well unfair». What's the truth? | Sorry doesn't cancel consequences, but it helps | A real sorry should cancel all punishments, every time | Teachers ignore every sorry | He shouldn't have said sorry
test: «Uncle sent your letter back?? That's so peak, Dad.» (peak is London slang for bad luck.) What did Dad say about it? | He'd done his part, the rest was up to his brother | He would send a letter every day forever | He'd never say sorry to anyone again | He'd make his brother forgive him
test: What would probably happen if Kieran's mum keeps making him say «it's fine» when it isn't? | He'd stay angry inside | He'd feel fine straight away | Connor would get angry instead | They'd be best friends at once
test: In a cartoon, someone says sorry and the other instantly hugs them. In real life? | It can take time | It always works instantly | Sorries are never accepted | You must hug after every sorry
test: «SORY» on a wonky cake. Why did it work? | It showed real effort, an action that said sorry | The spelling was perfect and the cake was really tasty | Aisha was very hungry | Cakes always end arguments
test: Lily forgave Maddie but won't tell her secrets for a while. Is that fair? | Yes, forgiving isn't the same as trusting straight away | No, forgiving means trusting fully at once | No, she should never forgive her | Yes, because Maddie is a bad person
test: Mr Grimshaw: «Apology accepted. Now the weeding.» Why add the weeding? | Sorry plus making it right is stronger | He wanted a helper in the garden forever, for free | He didn't accept the sorry | Weeding is a punishment for all kids
exam: Identify the principal constituents of a genuine apology as established in the lesson. | Direct address, a statement of the act and an acknowledgement of its wrongness | A gift of value accompanied by a smile | A statement regretting the other party's emotional response | A brief utterance delivered at the instruction of a parent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «Sorry you got upset»? | It attributes fault to the feelings of the injured party | It constitutes a complete and adequate apology in every respect | It acknowledges precisely the wrong committed | It is the most courteous form of apology
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of an apology upon a disciplinary sanction. | The sanction remains, though the apology may improve the regard in which the pupil is held | The sanction is automatically annulled | The sanction is doubled in consequence | The apology is disregarded entirely
exam: Ascertain the correct position where an apology, sincerely offered, is declined by the injured party. | Acceptance rests with the injured party and cannot be compelled | The injured party is obliged to accept it forthwith | The apology must be repeated until accepted | The apologising party is thereby released from all regard for the other
